<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.16.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02034</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.16.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>4fbbcbfc-c875-4601-8a9c-7d6f8f6d3a8d</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise id="3bdda842-a202-4cfa-96bf-c80303719329" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Enter your answer here:</para><para>What is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of the graph that represents \(y=(x−2)^2−8\)?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! 2. The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex is found by using the squared term of the vertex form. The value of the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of the graph is 2.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.16.2 – 7.16.4. </para><para>The correct answer is 2. The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex is found by using the squared term of the vertex form. The value of the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of the graph is 2. </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="b9a9a833-8d7c-4a05-be34-ce23d9a12115" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Enter your answer here:</para><para>What is the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of the graph that represents \(y=(x−2)^2−8\)? </para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! -8. In vertex form, the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex is the constant. The value of the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of the graph is -8.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.16.2 – 7.16.4.</para><para>The correct answer is -8. In vertex form, the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex is the constant. The value of the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of the graph is -8. </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="10697901-1d71-4c1e-8aa3-341cded90fed" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Enter your answer here:</para><para>Where is the \(y\)-intercept for the equation \(y=(x−2)^2−8\) located?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! -4. Substitute 0 for \(x\) and you get \(y=(0−2)^2−8=4−8=-4\).</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.16.2 – 7.16.4. </para><para>The correct answer is -4. Substitute 0 for \(x\) and you get \(y=(0−2)^2−8=4−8=-4\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="7614b261-dbad-48ee-a025-2f05bc4aa041" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Which of the following is another point on the graph of the equation \(y=(x−2)^2−8\)?</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\((4,-4)\)</para><para>\((-4,4)\)</para><para>\((4,4)\)</para><para>\((-4,-4)\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \((4,-4)\). The \(y\)-intercept is 2 horizontal units to the left of the vertical line containing the vertex (the axis of symmetry). Using the symmetry of the graph, another point with the same \(y\)-coordinate is located 2 horizontal units to the right of the vertical line containing the vertex.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.16.2.</para><para>The correct answer is: \((4,-4)\). The \(y\)-intercept is 2 horizontal units to the left of the vertical line containing the vertex (the axis of symmetry). Using the symmetry of the graph, another point with the same \(y\)-coordinate is located 2 horizontal units to the right of the vertical line containing the vertex. </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="51a7d19d-acae-4276-9c8c-dd6ad3831974" class="multiple-choice"><problem><newline/><para>Which graph is a representation of \(y=(x - 6)^2 - 5\)?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! Yes graph A.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.16.3 and 7.16.4.  </para><para>The correct answer is Yes. </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="57ca4658-384a-4e06-88db-22c107551c69" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Without graphing, determine if the vertex of the graph representing \(v\) shows the minimum or maximum value of the function.</para><para>The function \(v\) is defined by \(v(x)=12(x+5)^2−7\).</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>The vertex shows the minimum value.</para><para>The vertex shows the maximum value.</para></problem><solution><para> Correct! The vertex shows the minimum value. The \(a\)-value, or the coefficient of the squared term is positive, so the graph opens upward.This means the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex is the minimum value of the function. The vertex is at \((-5,-7)\). To verify your response, enter a couple of \(x\) -values on either side of -5 into the function. For example, find \(v(-6)\) and \(v(-4)\). Each time, the output is greater than \(v(-5)\).</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.2.</para><para>The correct answer is: The vertex shows the minimum value. The \(a\)-value, or the coefficient of the squared term is positive, so the graph opens upward.This means the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex is the minimum value of the function. The vertex is at \((-5,-7)\). To verify your response, enter a couple of \(x\) -values on either side of -5 into the function. For example, find \(v(-6)\) and \(v(-4)\). Each time, the output is greater than \(v(-5)\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="88f0ebcf-8946-42f3-9d92-5ccad7f2a77e" class="multiple-choice"><problem><newline/><para>Choose the equation that matches the graph of parabola D .</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(y=-2(x−6)^2−5\)</para><para>\(y=(x−6)^2−5\)</para><para>\(y=6(x−6)^2−5\)</para><para>\(y=-\frac {1}{3}(x−6)^2−5\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \(y=-\frac {1}{3}(x−6)^2−5\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.16.3. </para><para>The correct answer is: \(y=-\frac {1}{3}(x−6)^2−5\).
      </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="7786bff1-c50b-4411-b3e8-1b0fcf3eded0" class="multiple-choice"><problem><newline/><para>Choose the equation that matches the graph B.</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(y=-2(x−6)^2−5\)</para><para>\(y=(x−6)^2−5\)</para><para>\(y=6(x−6)^2−5\)</para><para>\(y=-\frac {1}{3}(x−6)^2−5\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct!</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.16.3.
      </para><para>The correct answer is: \(y=-2(x−6)^2−5\).
      </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="26117eea-4e73-4f7b-8e19-a0eecac697c2" class="multiple-choice"><problem><newline/><para>Choose the equation that matches the graph C.</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(y=-2(x−6)^2−5\)</para><para>\(y=(x−6)^2−5\)</para><para>\(y=6(x−6)^2−5\)</para><para>\(y=-\frac {1}{3}(x−6)^2−5\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct!</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.16.3. </para><para>The correct answer is: \(y=6(x−6)^2−5\)
      </para></solution></exercise></section></section>
